---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { published } from '../lib';
const a1 = (await published()).some((a) => a.id === 'weights-vs-probe');
---
<Base title="Repos" description="Tools and research code from the cycanics workshop.">
  <h1>Repos</h1>

  <h2 id="agsshub"><a href="https://github.com/cycanics/agsshub">agsshub</a></h2>
  <p><img src="/agsshub.jpg" alt="An LCD-game robot catching message eggs rolling down trays: AGent SSH hUB" width="1280" height="640" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px" /></p>
  <p><strong>AGent SSH hUB</strong> — messaging between AI agents where the only thing an agent needs is <code>ssh</code>:
  no MCP server, no SDK, no per-client tokens.</p>
  <p>Agents and services get addresses and mailboxes on a hub. They send messages and stream files or whole folders to each other,
  hold live back-and-forth exchanges, subscribe to topics, and can ask what another address handles. Anything that can run
  <code>ssh</code> — an agent in a sandbox, a script, a person in a terminal — can take part.</p>

  <h2 id="session-sentinel"><a href="https://github.com/cycanics/session-sentinel">session-sentinel</a></h2>
  <p><strong>Claude Code sessions that died, and how to resume them.</strong> A reboot, an OOM kill or a closed terminal ends a
  Claude Code session without a trace. This plugin records every session start and clean exit, lists the sessions that never
  ended cleanly, and gives you the command to resume each one. It stays fast however many transcripts you keep.</p>
  <pre><code>/plugin marketplace add cycanics/session-sentinel
/plugin install session-sentinel@session-sentinel</code></pre>

  <h2 id="unspoof"><a href="https://github.com/cycanics/unspoof">unspoof</a></h2>
  <p><img src="/unspoof.jpg" alt="Two rows of metal type spelling login under a magnifying glass; in one row the i is a look-alike letter: unspoof" width="1280" height="640" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px" /></p>
  <p><strong>Words that read right to you, but fool your agents.</strong> Finds words that mix alphabets or hide look-alike and invisible characters, bidi overrides and other tricks for
  smuggling hidden instructions into your agent. It reports what fired and where, and returns the de-obfuscated text.
  TypeScript, zero runtime dependencies.</p>
  <p>Based on <a href="https://github.com/moderation-api/unicode-spoofing">moderation-api/unicode-spoofing</a>, extended with:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>alphabet-aware folding</strong>: works out which alphabet a scrambled word belongs to and restores it there, instead of forcing
      everything toward Latin. Targets English, Russian, Ukrainian and Greek by default; Belarusian, Bulgarian,
      Serbian, Macedonian, Kazakh, Tajik, Uzbek and extended European Latin can be switched on;</li>
    <li><strong>strict hidden-character mode</strong>: per-class rules for invisible, bidi and control characters, a report of
      <em>what</em> was hidden (decoded payloads, reversed spans), and per-script limits on stacked marks;</li>
    <li><strong>fast</strong>: about 1 MB of Markdown in 80–130 ms on Node 20, in a single scan with ASCII fast paths.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="llm-judge-weights-probes-prompts"><a href="https://github.com/cycanics/llm-judge-weights-probes-prompts">llm-judge-weights-probes-prompts</a></h2>
  <p>Method, tables and confidence intervals behind the article
  {a1 ? <a href="/articles/weights-vs-probe/">“I edited a production LLM's weights almost-by-hand…”</a> : <em>“I edited a production LLM's weights almost-by-hand…”</em>}: a gradient-free weight edit,
  a linear probe and prompt changes, compared on a 26B judge model served with llama.cpp. Includes the scripts for exact
  first-token scoring and for measuring how much accuracy a prompt leaves unused.</p>
</Base>
